export const FROST=`Two roads diverged in a yellow wood,
And sorry I could not travel both
And be one traveler, long I stood
And looked down one as far as I could
To where it bent in the undergrowth;

Then took the other, as just as fair,
And having perhaps the better claim,
Because it was grassy and wanted wear;
Though as for that the passing there
Had worn them really about the same,

And both that morning equally lay
In leaves no step had trodden black.
Oh, I kept the first for another day!
Yet knowing how way leads on to way,
I doubted if I should ever come back.

I shall be telling this with a sigh
Somewhere ages and ages hence:
Two roads diverged in a wood, and I--
I took the one less traveled by,
And that has made all the difference.`;
export const STORY=`The small robot lived beside a green door. Each morning it opened the door and looked at the sky. If the sky was blue, it walked to the garden. If the sky was gray, it stayed in the room and read a book.
One morning the robot found a red seed. It put the seed in a little pot and gave it water. The next morning it gave it water again. Day after day, the robot waited.
At last a green leaf appeared. The robot opened the green door and carried the pot into the garden. It put the pot beside a yellow flower. The sky was blue and the garden was warm.
When the rain came, the robot brought the book outside. It read beside the green door while the leaves drank the rain. A bird landed on the pot. The robot watched the bird and forgot the book.
The next day another leaf appeared. The robot had learned to wait. The bird had learned where to find a quiet place. And the little seed had become a small green plant.`;
export const PATTERN=('red blue green. red blue green. one two three. one two three.\n').repeat(14);
export const LESSONS=[
 {title:'A language model plays a guessing game.',short:'The next-token game',group:'THE INGREDIENTS',intro:'Give it some text. Ask what comes next. Do this again and again, and a sequence of tiny predictions becomes a sentence.',task:'Choose what should come after “Two roads diverged in a yellow”.',why:'Your experience makes “wood” feel likely. A model starts with random numbers. Training nudges those numbers until useful patterns emerge.',technical:'A language model estimates P(next token | previous tokens). For a sequence x₁…xₙ, it models P(x₁…xₙ) as the product of conditional next-token probabilities. Our actual model predicts one character at a time; this opening word game is an illustration.',check:['Does generating text normally update model weights?','No. It uses the weights already learned.','Yes. Every answer is a training step.'],answer:0},
 {title:'First, choose what it can learn from.',short:'Text & data',group:'',intro:'A model learns patterns from examples. Our first dataset is a single poem: small enough to inspect, and small enough to overfit.',task:'Edit the text or choose another dataset. Then create a fresh model.',why:'A single poem can teach spelling patterns and fragments of lines. It cannot teach broad knowledge, reliable reasoning, or conversation.',technical:'Split the sequence before extracting overlapping windows. We train only on the first portion and evaluate on the last portion. Windows never cross that boundary. The character vocabulary is built from the entire selected text (including held-out characters); no held-out targets are optimized. This is a teaching holdout, not an independent benchmark. The repeating-pattern preset deliberately repeats examples across both splits: low held-out loss there demonstrates pattern learning, not general language ability. In larger systems: establish provenance and permission, clean and deduplicate, filter quality and sensitive data, then keep evaluation sources separate.',check:['Why hold some text back from training?','To make the vocabulary smaller.','To test performance on examples not optimized during training.'],answer:1},
 {title:'Turn text into a language of numbers.',short:'Tokens & vocabulary',group:'',intro:'Computers do not receive words as meanings. A tokenizer cuts text into pieces and gives each distinct piece an integer ID.',task:'Click a token. Compare characters, words, and a small pair-merging experiment.',why:'“The” might be one token in a large model. Here it is three characters. Neither an ID nor its numerical size carries meaning by itself.',technical:'Our model uses Unicode code points, not bytes or UTF-16 code units. IDs index learned embedding rows. The word and BPE views are teaching sandboxes only. In byte-pair encoding, frequent adjacent pairs are repeatedly merged; a production tokenizer learns merges from a large corpus and may start from bytes, use pre-tokenization, and reserve special tokens. Our merge toy starts from the displayed snippet and omits those conventions.',check:['If token A has ID 12 and token B has ID 13, are they similar?','Yes, their IDs are close.','The IDs alone tell us nothing about similarity.'],answer:1},
 {title:'One line becomes many practice questions.',short:'Context & targets',group:'',intro:'For every input position, the answer is the next character. Slide the window to see how one stretch of text supplies a whole training example.',task:'Move the window. Compare the input row with its targets, shifted by one.',why:'The model may see the current input character while predicting the following character. It must never peek ahead at its target.',technical:'A sequence of T input tokens uses T shifted labels: inputs = ids[s:s+T], labels = ids[s+1:s+T+1]. Teacher forcing supplies the real earlier characters during training. Causal masking allows all T positions to be trained in one forward pass. At generation time the next character is unknown, so we append one sampled character and repeat. Our batches average gradients from four separately evaluated windows.',check:['At input position i, which label is predicted?','The character at i + 1.','The same character at i.'],answer:0},
 {title:'Give each token a learnable vector.',short:'Embeddings & position',group:'INSIDE THE TRANSFORMER',intro:'An embedding is a row of adjustable numbers. Add another vector for position, so the same character can play a different role in a different place.',task:'Select a character and a position. Inspect the actual vectors being added.',why:'These numbers begin random and learn through the same prediction loss as the rest of the model. In this tiny model, they should not be read as a map of human concepts.',technical:'For token ID t at position i: xᵢ = E[t] + P[i]. E has shape vocabulary × d_model and P has shape context × d_model. Position indices restart at zero for each training window and cropped generation window. This implementation learns absolute position embeddings. Many larger models instead use rotary positional embeddings (RoPE); the original transformer used sinusoidal positions.',check:['What happens to a token embedding during training?','It stays a fixed one-hot label.','Its vector is updated by gradients.'],answer:1},
 {title:'Attention is a weighted conversation.',short:'Queries, keys & values',group:'',intro:'A query asks what to look for. Keys decide how strongly to match. Values carry the information that gets mixed together.',task:'Drag the query or a key. Change a value. Watch the scores and mixture respond.',why:'Attention does not fetch a whole word unchanged. It forms a weighted sum of value vectors. All three projections are learned from the next-token task.',technical:'Q = XWq + bq, K = XWk + bk, V = XWv + bv. Each head uses softmax(QKᵀ / √d_head + causal_mask)V, normalized separately for each query row. Disallowed future scores act like −∞, giving zero weight. The diagonal is allowed. Heads are concatenated and projected by Wo. Our drag experiment uses 2D hand-set vectors and scalar values; the actual transformer uses learned vectors and always keeps the causal mask on. Attention weights alone are not a complete explanation of model behavior.',check:['What changes if you edit a value but keep queries and keys fixed?','The attention weights.','The output mixture, while attention weights stay the same.'],answer:1},
 {title:'Put the whole transformer together.',short:'The transformer block',group:'',intro:'Attention lets positions exchange information. A small neural network transforms each position. Shortcut connections keep earlier information in the stream.',task:'Click each stage to follow real tensor shapes and numbers from your model.',why:'This is the actual path used for training and generation. Every embedding, projection, normalization parameter, and output weight is trainable.',technical:'We use one pre-normalized decoder block: x → LayerNorm → multi-head attention → add x → LayerNorm → Linear(d,4d) → GELU → Linear(4d,d) → residual add → final LayerNorm → vocabulary projection. LayerNorm normalizes the d features at each position, then applies learned scale γ and offset β, with ε = 10⁻⁵. GELU is a smooth nonlinear activation; without nonlinearities, stacked linear layers could collapse into one linear map. We use the tanh GELU approximation. No dropout, weight decay, weight tying, or KV cache is used.',check:['What does a residual connection do?','Adds an earlier representation to a transformed one.','Deletes all earlier information.'],answer:0},
 {title:'Scores become probabilities. Mistakes get a cost.',short:'Probabilities & loss',group:'',intro:'The model outputs one score, called a logit, for every possible next token. Softmax turns those scores into probabilities that add up to one.',task:'Raise the correct token’s score and watch its cross-entropy loss fall.',why:'A confident wrong guess receives a larger penalty than an uncertain one. Loss gives the training process a precise quantity to reduce.',technical:'pᵢ = exp(zᵢ − max(z)) / Σⱼ exp(zⱼ − max(z)). Subtracting the maximum prevents overflow without changing probabilities. Cross-entropy for target y is −ln(p_y), measured in nats. We average it over positions and then over the batch. The logit gradient is (pᵢ − 1[i=y]) / T. Character perplexity = exp(mean character loss); compare it only for the same tokenizer and evaluation data. Token probability is not a probability that a factual claim is true.',check:['What happens to loss when the target probability approaches 1?','It approaches zero.','It approaches infinity.'],answer:0},
 {title:'Learning means moving the numbers.',short:'Gradients & the optimizer',group:'LEARN & EXPERIMENT',intro:'Backpropagation finds how each weight affects loss. An optimizer uses those gradients to make small changes, then tries another batch.',task:'Take a gradient step on this simple curve. Try a learning rate that is too large.',why:'The curve below is a one-weight illustration. Your real model has thousands of interacting weights; the training room uses their actual gradients.',technical:'Reverse-mode automatic differentiation follows the chain rule backward through the recorded operations. Shared paths add gradients; repeated token IDs scatter-add into embedding gradients. Plain SGD uses w ← w − η∂L/∂w. Our real model uses Adam: m ← .9m + .1g; v ← .999v + .001g²; w ← w − η m̂/(√v̂ + 10⁻⁸), with bias-corrected m̂ and v̂. We average four windows, clip the global gradient norm to 1, and update once. Adam state and its step counter are saved with checkpoints.',check:['What does a gradient describe?','How a tiny parameter change affects loss locally.','The best possible value of a parameter.'],answer:0},
 {title:'Train your own tiny language model.',short:'The training room',group:'',intro:'These curves come from real forward passes, backpropagation, and weight updates running on your device. Nothing is sent to an AI service.',task:'Run 100 steps. Then try 500 more and compare training loss with held-out loss.',why:'Training loss should fall. Held-out loss may stop improving or rise as the model memorizes. One short poem gives a particularly noisy, narrow test.',technical:'Each optimizer step samples four windows from the training portion, averages their gradients, clips the global norm to 1, and applies Adam. Every 10 updates we evaluate 12 deterministic windows from each split; the plotted train curve is evaluation on training text, not the most recent random batch. The same evaluation windows are reused for comparability. The uniform baseline is ln(vocabulary size), not a learned unigram baseline. The worker yields between chunks so you can pause. Browsers vary in speed; start with width 16 on a slower phone.',check:['Training loss falls while held-out loss rises. What might be happening?','The model must be broken.','It may be overfitting its training examples.'],answer:1},
 {title:'Look inside a real prediction.',short:'Inspect the model',group:'',intro:'Type a context, choose a head, and inspect which earlier characters it mixes at each position. These are your model’s actual attention weights.',task:'Select a row in the heatmap. Compare heads before and after training.',why:'Heads may learn different patterns, but they are not assigned jobs such as “grammar” or “meaning.” A bright square is a large mixing weight, not proof of reasoning.',technical:'Rows are query positions; columns are key/value positions. Each permitted row sums to 1 and the future triangle is exactly zero. The output is H concatenated weighted mixtures, followed by the learned output projection. Only the last context position provides logits for the next generated character. For contexts longer than the model’s limit, we retain only the most recent characters and restart learned absolute positions within that window.',check:['Why is the upper-right triangle empty?','The model has not been trained enough.','Those positions are in the future and are masked.'],answer:1},
 {title:'Let it write, one character at a time.',short:'Generate & save',group:'',intro:'Predict a distribution, choose a character, append it, and repeat. Generation uses learned weights; it does not train them.',task:'Generate one character, then a longer continuation. Try a warmer or colder temperature.',why:'A model trained on a single poem usually produces fragments, repetition, and invented spellings. Better sampling cannot supply knowledge missing from training.',technical:'For temperature τ > 0, sample from softmax(logits/τ). At τ = 0, choose the highest logit greedily. Top-k retains the k highest logits before normalization; 0 keeps all. Top-p (not implemented here) retains a variable-size prefix whose cumulative probability reaches a threshold. A separate sampling seed leaves the training RNG untouched. This model recomputes the whole cropped context each step; large deployments commonly use a KV cache. Export includes architecture, vocabulary-defining text, weights, Adam moments, RNG state, and training history. Import validates shapes and finite numbers.',check:['What does increasing temperature change?','The learned weights.','The sampling distribution.'],answer:1},
 {title:'How does this become a useful assistant?',short:'From tiny model to LLM',group:'THE BIGGER PICTURE',intro:'You have built the core mechanism. Useful assistants add much more data and compute, specialized training, careful evaluation, and systems around the model.',task:'Match a goal to the operation that would actually help.',why:'Scaling changes capability, but it does not turn next-token probabilities into guarantees. Evaluate the behavior you care about, including errors and uncertainty.',technical:'This is a decoder-only, one-block language model. Encoders use bidirectional context for representation tasks; encoder–decoders combine an input encoder with an autoregressive decoder. Modern systems may use RoPE, RMSNorm, gated FFNs, grouped-query attention, mixture-of-experts, and distributed training. Large-scale training adds precision management, scheduled learning rates, checkpoint recovery, and parallelism. Deployment adds batching, KV caches, quantization, and latency/memory evaluation. Instruction tuning and preference optimization change weights; prompting, retrieval, and tool results supply context. None of these removes the need for held-out task evaluations, contamination checks, robustness testing, and monitoring.',check:['Does ordinary retrieval-augmented generation retrain the model for every query?','Yes. Retrieved documents become weight updates.','No. Retrieved passages usually become additional context.'],answer:1},
];
